## The idea

We do not need to actually delete leaves. A node is removed in round **h**, where h is its **height** counted from the bottom: leaves have height 0, their parents 1, and so on (height = 1 + the larger height of its children). So one post-order walk computes every node's height and drops the node into the list for that round. The N-ary version is identical — just loop over all children.

## Step by step

1. Height(null) = −1, so a leaf gets 1 + (−1) = 0.
2. Height(node) = 1 + max(Height(left), Height(right)), computed after the children (post-order).
3. If this is the first node with that height, add a new list for that round.
4. Add the node's value to rounds[height].
5. N-ary: height = 1 + the largest height among all children (−1 if there are none).

## Worked example

**Input:** 1 has children 2 and 3; 2 has children 4 and 5

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| node 4 | leaf → height 0 → round 0 = [4] |
| node 5 | leaf → height 0 → round 0 = [4, 5] |
| node 2 | 1 + max(0, 0) = 1 → round 1 = [2] |
| node 3 | leaf → height 0 → round 0 = [4, 5, 3] |
| node 1 | 1 + max(1, 0) = 2 → round 2 = [1] |

**Result:** [[4, 5, 3], [2], [1]]

## Cost

O(n) time — every node visited once. O(h) call-stack depth for the recursion, plus O(n) for the output.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// TreeNode is provided (Models.cs): Value, Left, Right.
public static class LeafPeeling {

    public static List<List<int>> PeelLevels(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<int>> rounds = new List<List<int>>();
        Height(root, rounds);
        return rounds;
    }

    // Returns the node's height (leaf = 0) and records it in the matching round.
    private static int Height(TreeNode node, List<List<int>> rounds) {
        if (node == null) return -1;

        int leftHeight = Height(node.Left, rounds);
        int rightHeight = Height(node.Right, rounds);
        int height = 1 + Math.Max(leftHeight, rightHeight);

        if (height == rounds.Count) rounds.Add(new List<int>());   // first node in this round
        rounds[height].Add(node.Value);
        return height;
    }
}

// Follow-up: a node may have any number of children.
public class NaryNode {
    public int Value;
    public List<NaryNode> Children = new List<NaryNode>();
    public NaryNode(int value) { Value = value; }
}

public static class NaryLeafPeeling {

    public static List<List<int>> PeelLevels(NaryNode root) {
        List<List<int>> rounds = new List<List<int>>();
        Height(root, rounds);
        return rounds;
    }

    private static int Height(NaryNode node, List<List<int>> rounds) {
        if (node == null) return -1;

        int tallestChild = -1;
        foreach (NaryNode child in node.Children) {
            int h = Height(child, rounds);
            if (h > tallestChild) tallestChild = h;
        }
        int height = tallestChild + 1;

        if (height == rounds.Count) rounds.Add(new List<int>());
        rounds[height].Add(node.Value);
        return height;
    }
}
```

